      "answer": "Method B results in a product with a much longer shelf life at ambient temperature.",
      "explanation": "Method A (sun-drying) is a preservation technique that works by removing moisture to inhibit microbial growth. While effective, dried fish is still susceptible to moisture re-absorption, pests, and quality degradation over time. Method B describes canning. In this process, the fish is sealed in an airtight container (the can) and then subjected to high temperatures (retorting/sterilization). This intense heat treatment kills virtually all spoilage microorganisms (bacteria, yeasts, molds) and deactivates enzymes. Because the can is hermetically sealed, the product is protected from re-contamination. The result is a commercially sterile product that has an exceptionally long shelf life (often several years) without needing refrigeration. This is a far more robust and long-lasting preservation method than simple drying. Canning is a high-tech, capital-intensive process."
