      "explanation": "In the wet (or washed) processing method, the outer skin and pulp of the coffee cherry are first removed by a machine (pulping). This leaves the coffee bean encased in its parchment layer, which is covered by a slippery, sugary substance called mucilage. This mucilage is difficult to remove mechanically. The beans are then placed in fermentation tanks with water for 12-48 hours. During this time, naturally present microbes and enzymes break down the complex pectins in the mucilage layer, making it soluble and easy to wash off. The primary purpose of this fermentation is purely to remove this mucilage layer cleanly. The characteristic roasted coffee flavors are developed later during the roasting process, not during fermentation. After fermentation, the beans are thoroughly washed before being dried."
