      "explanation": "The diagram depicts bench terracing, a major engineering practice for controlling water erosion on steep slopes. It involves constructing a series of level or nearly level platforms, or 'benches', that cut into the hillside, resembling a staircase. This fundamentally changes the topography of the slope. By converting one long, steep slope into a series of shorter, level steps, terracing dramatically reduces the velocity and erosive power of water runoff. It allows water to be retained on each bench, giving it ample time to infiltrate the soil, thereby conserving both soil and water. This is a highly effective, but labor-intensive, method for making steep land suitable for cultivation. Contour farming follows the slope's natural curve without creating level steps, and a shelterbelt is for wind erosion."
