      "answer": "Including an untreated control plot and randomly assigning replicated plots for each fungicide treatment within the field.",
      "explanation": "A scientifically valid experiment requires specific design principles to ensure the results are reliable and not due to chance or other confounding factors. The most essential components here are controls, replication, and randomization. 1) An 'untreated control' plot (receiving no fungicide) is crucial to measure the baseline level of disease pressure and to confirm that both fungicides are actually having an effect compared to doing nothing. 2) 'Replication' (having multiple plots for each treatment) is necessary to account for natural field variability and to allow for statistical analysis of the results. 3) 'Randomization' of the plot locations helps to eliminate any systematic bias due to variations in soil, drainage, or other factors across the field. Comparing to a previous year is invalid because weather and disease pressure change year-to-year. Applying them on different days introduces a time variable. A mixture doesn't allow for a comparison of the individual fungicides."
