      "explanation": "'Elite capture' is a concept in development economics and political science that describes a situation where public resources or programs, intended for the poor, are diverted or disproportionately benefit the wealthy and powerful. In the context of fertilizer subsidies, this can happen in several ways. Larger, more influential farmers may have better access to information and transportation, allowing them to acquire large quantities of the subsidized fertilizer. Politically connected individuals or corrupt officials might control the distribution network, diverting the fertilizer to be sold on the black market at a higher price. This means the intended beneficiaries – poor, remote smallholders – may not be able to access the subsidy at all, while the 'elites' capture the economic benefit. It is a major challenge in designing and implementing equitable and effective subsidy programs."
