      "explanation": "The equimarginal principle (or principle of equal marginal returns) is a core economic concept for optimizing resource allocation among competing uses. It states that to maximize total profit, a limited resource (like money or labor) should be distributed among different enterprises in such a way that the marginal return—the additional profit generated by the last unit (e.g., the last dollar) of the resource invested—is equal for all enterprises. If the last dollar spent on maize returns $1.50 in profit, while the last dollar on soybeans returns only $1.10, the farmer should shift spending from soybeans to maize until the marginal returns are equalized. This ensures that every dollar is being used in its most profitable capacity, thereby maximizing the total profit from all enterprises combined."
